  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you put in all that effort and your log home interior stain just doesn't turn out like you pictured, right? Often, it comes down to a few common blunders. A big one is trying to stain over an incompatible or film-forming coating, which can lead to peeling and uneven color. Or maybe you only spot-prepped certain areas, so the stain absorbed differently and now you've got a splotchy mess. Using products that seal the wood too tightly, instead of letting it breathe, can also mess things up over time. It's really important to make sure the wood is properly cleaned and totally dry before you even think about applying stain. And always, always test your chosen stain in a hidden spot first to make sure you love the look and it behaves well with your logs. If you're seeing widespread peeling, large faded areas, or a mix of old coatings, that's usually a sign it’s time to call in a professional. They can help make those tricky decisions about sanding and removal to ensure you get a beautiful, uniform finish. Don't let a bad stain job ruin the charm of your log home – get the right help if it feels overwhelming!",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
